---- Setting up the VM in Proxmox ----

1. In Proxmox > >  Local (DeviceName) > CT Templates > Ubuntu ISO > Download it

2. **IMPORTANT: During Setup**
  2a. CT ID: 100
  2b. Hostname: pihole
  2c. Password
  2d. Uncheck "Unprivilged container"
  2e. Choose the Ubuntu template
      2f. RAM: 512 Mb
      2g. Disk size: 4 GB
      2h. Cores: 1
            2i. Bridge: vmbr0
            2j. IPv4: Static
            2k. Ipv4/CIDR: 192.168.x.x/24    (( #PiHole needs its own IP / And check the gateways IP range ))
            2l. Gateway 192.168.1.1
            2m. DNS: Leave as default
            2n. Finish

3. Starting the container
  3a. (Optional) Start at boot
      3a. Options > Start at Boot > Yes
  3b. Open the container > Console > Login in as: root
      3b. Type: curl -sSL https://install.pi-hole.net | bash
          3c. During the installation setup
              3c. Upstream DNS: Google or CloudFlare
              3c. Block List: Leave default checked
              3c. Install web interface: Yes
              3c. Install lighttpd: Yes
              3c. Log queries: Yes
              3c. Privacy Mode: Show everything
(Once the setup is done, just note the IP address and you will not need to interact with this anymore)

4. Accessing PiHole
  4a. On the browser: http://192.168.x.x/admin
  Log in with root and the password you made

5. Setting the PiHole to the DNS
  5a. Go to: http://192.168.1.1
  5b. DNS Settings (LAN or DHCP settings)
  5c. Set Primary DNS / Custom Server to PiHole IP address
  5d. Set Secondary DNS to 8.8.8.8 or the backup PiHole IP address
  5e. Save it

(Backup PiHole is the same process)

___________________________________________________________________________________________________________________

**If its not sunshine and rainbow....
(The PiHole Container won't load in / its just not working at all)
(Sometimes the container doesn't have the IP address at all...)


1.Go into the Server terminal from Proxmox
  1a. nano /etc/pve/lxc/101.conf
      1b. Add these 2 lines
          unprivileged: 0
          features: nesting=1
  1a. Enter: pct enter 100 

2. Now you're in the PiHole container
  2a. Run: ip a
      (this checks your IP address, you most likely won't have one)

3. Setup the ip address
  3a. ip addr add 192.168.x.x/24 dev eth0
  3b. ip link set eth0 up
  3c. ip route add default  via 192.168.1.1
3a. Making the IP address permanent
  3a. /etc/network/interfaces
        auto eth0
        iface eth0 inet static
              address 192.168.x.x/24
              gateway 192.168.1.1
              dns-nameservers 192.168.1.1
          (Crtl + X > Y > Enter > (to save it)

4. Now that you have IP address and internet
  4a. apt update && apt upgrade -y
  4b. apt install curl -y

5. Disable systemd-resolved
  5a. systemctl disable systemd-resolved
  5b. systemctl stop systemd-resolved

6. Install Pihole
  6a. curl -sSL https://install.pi-hole.net | bash
6.1. The setup
              6a    Upstream DNS: Google or CloudFlare
              6b. Block List: Leave default checked
              6c. Install web interface: Yes
              6e. Install lighttpd: Yes
              6f. Log queries: Yes
              6g. Privacy Mode: Show everything

7. Make the directory
  7a. mkdir -p /var/log/pihole
  7b. touch /var/log/pihole/FTL.log
  7c. touch /var/log/pihole/pihole.log
  7d. chmod 777 /var/log/pihole

